  "query_preview": "..(Sunday through Friday)\nIssued at 438 PM EDT FRI SEP 18 2026\n\nThe models are in decent agreement with an overall amplified \npattern over the CONUS through the long term portion of the \nforecast. On Sunday, a ridge will be centered over the Ark-La-Tex \nregion, with zonal flow spread from the Great Lakes/Ohio Valley \nthrough New England. Upstream, a dampening closed low will be \nmoving through the northern Rockies, with a broad short wave \ntrough aligned south through the Four Corners region. The models \nare in good agreement into early next week with the Ark-La-Tex \nridge continuing to retreat southwest and weaken with time, while \nthe upper level low/short wave trough translates east from the \ncentral/northern Plains to the Midwest region. The pattern then \nturns more blocky through the middle and latter portion of the \nwork week, with a broader closed low potentially taking shape over\nthe Great Lakes/Ohio Valley region, while ridging reasserts \nitself across the southern Plains. The closed low will continue to\nbroaden and expand towards the Eastern Seaboard through the end of\nthe period. Confidence is lower on the details, as model guidance\ntypically does not handle larger cutoff evolutions very well. \n\nSensible weather for eastern Kentucky will feature gradually\nrelenting heat for the second half of the weekend and early next\nweek, with high temperatures near and above 90 mainly reserved for\nlocations near and southwest of I-75. Some convection will also\nthreaten Sunday and especially on Monday, as another frontal \nboundary drops south across the Ohio Valley and likely stalls in \nthe vicinity of our area. Some stronger storms and locally heavy\nrainfall will be possible, although higher PWATs will likely\nremain concentrated more so just north of the Ohio River.\nUnsettled weather will continue through the middle of the work\nweek, as the upper level low holds sway aloft. Thankfully, these \nlower 500 mb heights will also result in a welcomed cooler air \nmass to take hold over the region. Highs will retreat back to the\n70s each day from Wednesday through Friday, with rain chances \ndiminishing Thursday into Friday, as the low gradually pulls to \nour east.",
